On a low grey rock outcrop beyond the middle-distance coral wall, Maëlle — an adult mermaid with long wavy dark-teal hair falling to mid-back, pale green eyes, wearing a fitted sleeveless pale sea-green cotton top and a single shimmering blue-green tail with a wide flared fin — sits above the water and lifts a brass hand bell with a domed body and loop handle high, ringing it into the clear afternoon air. Far back on the wet sand of the shore in the foreground, Léa — a toddler girl with dark brown chin-length hair, wearing an orange sweater and blue leggings — and Jules — a school-age boy with dark brown hair, wearing a red hoodie and grey jogger trousers — stand together side by side, seen from behind as they watch the rock, both with heads lifted, shoulders high, and happy smiles. The ultra-wide view stretches from the wet sand across the coral wall with its coral arch to the rock, where the reef reaches outward to the calm sea.

