In the dim underwater shadows by the lip of the coral arch, Léa — a toddler girl with chin-length straight dark brown hair, brown eyes, wearing a long-sleeve green swim shirt and purple mermaid tail — floats close to the coral wall, crying as she presses both small balled fists against her eyes, her brows tightly pinched and her mouth trembling open in sorrow. Beside her on the sandy floor, Jules — a young school-age boy with combed short dark brown hair, blue eyes, wearing a short-sleeve blue swim shirt and green mermaid tail — holds both hands up in front of his chest, palms turned toward his face, looking down with sad lowered brows and an unhappy pout at his scraped palms coated with pale coral crumbs. Broken orange coral fragments rest in the sand hollow beneath the arch lip, surrounded by deep blue twilight water. Close-up framing cropped above their waists.

