From behind Léa — seen over her shoulder, cropped at the collar of her long-sleeve green swim shirt showing her loose chin-length dark brown hair — Léa swims slowly through the water with her purple tail gliding gently behind, both hands held open and palms flat outward in a calm greeting toward the coral arch. Across a few paces of water in the center of the arch, Gaspard — an olive-brown moray eel with cream-yellow spots, chest-high in scale, large dark round eyes, and a soft snout — rests within the opening, watching Léa with calm level brows, wide round dark eyes, and a closed relaxed mouth. The pale orange and rose coral arch rises over the sandy hollow, where broken coral crumbs lie undisturbed under the sunlit blue water.

